{"study_accession":"PRJDB15836","secondary_study_accession":"DRP009999","title":"Elucidating the pathogenesis of multifactorial diseases through comprehensive analysis of functional gene polymorphisms","description":"Genome-wide association studies (GWAS) have been conducted in multifactorial diseases such as autoimmune diseases, and more than 1000 disease susceptibility gene regions have been identified. However, GWAS only indicates the presence of disease-causing gene polymorphisms within a region. The effects of the accumulation of these gene polymorphisms on the functions of cells and tissues involved in disease states must be clarified. In this study, we aim to integrate existing disease GWAS data and eQTL and sQTL analyses to elucidate the pathogenesis of various multifactorial diseases through a comprehensive analysis of functional gene polymorphisms.The Data Access Committee of the Database Center for Life Science (DBCLS) approved that this personal data was made published according to the NBDC Guidelines for Human Data Sharing (https://humandbs.dbcls.jp/en/guidelines/data-sharing-guidelines) as the NBDC Research ID hum0312.v2 and the application ID J-DS000719-001.","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":29,"study_labels":[],"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMD00604073","study_accession":"PRJDB15836","blood_class":"pbmc","class_from":"sample","title":"PBMC from healthy control","description":"PBMC from healthy control","sex":"female","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2023-06-05","attributes":[["collection_date","missing"],["isolate","Monoisolate"],["INSDC secondary accession","DRS304544"],["NCBI submission package","Human.1.0"],["organism","Homo sapiens"],["sex","female"],["geo_loc_name","missing"],["tissue","peripheral blood"],["age","42"],["biomaterial_provider","Department of Genomic Function and Diversity, Medical Research Institute, Tokyo Medical and Dental University"],["sample_name","PBMC"],["BioSampleModel","Human